pponents' discards and melds:  \nNext player melds: J1 pung, B4 pung. Their history includes discards: T7, B7, B9, B2, B9, F2, J3, T4, T2, T3, B3, T8, T1. They discarded T8 once. So T8 might be safe to discard? But they also discarded T7, T4, etc. T6 has not been discarded by anyone? Looking at history:  \nYour history: you discarded T7, T1, T8, T5 etc.  \nNext player: discarded T8 once.  \nAcross: discarded T6 once? Actually across history: Play T6 (discarded T6). So T6 has been discarded.  \nPrevious player: no T6 or T8 discards.  \n\nSo both T6 and T8 have been discarded, so relatively safe. But which is more dangerous? Unshown T6: 3 left, T8: 2 left. Fewer left might be riskier for giving someone a pung? Actually if someone has a pair, they could pung. But they have melds already, so less likely.  \n\nAlso consider your hand potential: If you discard T6, you keep T8 which with 2 left might pair easier? Actually T8 has 2 left in wall, so chance to draw T8 is 2/?? total wall tiles unknown. T6 has 3 left. So T6 has better chance to draw a pair. So maybe keep T6 and discard T8.  \n\nBut also consider if you need T6 for chow with something? No.  \n\nGiven you have W9 pung, you can use that as a meld, and then you need a pair and one more meld from remaining 5 tiles. If you keep W2,W3,W4 as chow, that's one meld, then you have T6 and T8 left, which need to become a pair. So you are waiting to draw either T6 or T8 to complete the pair, and then you win if you have the chow and pung. That is a single wait for a pair. That's a common waiting shape. So after discarding one tile, you will have 7 hand tiles (since you discard one from 8, left 7). Those 7 should be arranged as: W9 pung (3 tiles), W2,W3,W4 chow (3 tiles), and one tile (either T6 or T8) which will be paired when you draw its duplicate. So you need to discard either T6 or T8 now, and keep the other as the waiting tile. Which one to keep? Since T6 has 3 left and T8 has 2 left, T6 has better chance to draw. So keep T6, discard T8.  \n\nCheck: If you discard T8, your hand becomes: W2,W3,W4, W9,W9,W9, T6. That's 7 tiles. You need to draw a T6 to pair and win. Also you could draw W1 or W5 to complete chow with W2,W3,W4? Actually W2,W3,W4 is already a complete chow, no need additional tile. So you only need a pair. So waiting for T6. If you draw T6, you have: chow (W2,W3,W4), pung (W9,W9,W9), pair (T6,T6), plus your two exposed chows. That's a winning hand.  \n\nAlternatively, if you discard T6, keep T8, you wait for T8. T8 has fewer copies, so less chance.  \n\nTherefore, discard T8.  \n\nAlso consider if you discard W9, you could have pair W9 and then need another meld, but that seems less efficient.  \n\nSo final choice: Play T8.  \n\nReason: Keeping the W2-W3-W4 chow and W9 pung maximizes hand structure, leaving a single wait for T6 pair, which has 3 unseen tiles for better probability. Discarding T8 is relatively safe as it has been discarded before.\n\nAnswer: Play T8"}
